WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:06.590
Following the smash-hit Skyfall, Daniel Craig
is back for his fourth outing as 007.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.481
I'm Jan and this is 25 things you probably
didn't know about SPECTRE.

00:00:10.481 --> 00:00:17.370
Spectre is the first time Aston Martin has
built a car specifically for a Bond movie.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:21.359
The previous Aston Martins that have appeared
in the franchise were available for public

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:22.359
purchase.

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:27.429
The car Aston Martin built for Spectre is
the DB10, which is a concept car with a chassis

00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:32.740
based on a modified V8 Vantage, and has a
top speed of 190mph.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:36.660
A total of ten were built, eight of which
were used for filming, while the other two

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.280
were used for promotion.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:44.180
As for the Jaguar C-X75 chasing Bond in Rome,
the car was so powerful that the stunt team

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.840
had to tone down its engine so the throttle
response was less aggressive.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.309
It took 3 weeks to film the car chase through
the streets of Rome, which involved shutting

00:00:54.309 --> 00:00:58.480
down key parts of the city while stunt performers
drove up to 100mph.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.280
Although the chase involves only two cars
on screen, the filmmakers actually used 8

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.500
Aston Martins and 7 Jaguars to shoot the scene.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.320
The filmmakers also shot for four days at
Rome's Museum of Roman Civilization, which

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.729
doubled as a cemetery.

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:19.140
The SPECTRE meeting in Rome was actually filmed
at Pinewood Studios on a set inspired by the

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.130
Royal Palace of Caserta, north of Naples.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.659
And Oxfordshire's Blenheim Palace doubled
for the exterior of the building where that

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:28.400
meeting takes place.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.310
The centrepiece of the movie's Day Of The
Dead parade is the Calavera Catrina skeleton,

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.230
which has a 10-metre wide hat and was inspired
by an etching by Mexican illustrator José

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.840
Guadalupe Posada.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.340
It took 107 make-up artists three and a half
hours each day to get over 1,500 extras ready

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.579
for filming.

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:54.229
75 costumers designed 1,500 different outfits,
including 6 paper dresses which took 3 weeks

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:59.210
each to make, and a team of 20 people was
needed to clean and repair costumes overnight

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.880
for the next day's shoot.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.310
Filming took place in three locations in Mexico
City: The Gran Hotel, the Plaza Tolsá, and

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:11.570
the city's main square, the Zócalo, where
Red Bull aerobatic pilot Chuck Aaron performed

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:17.140
a helicopter stunt, flying only 30 feet above
the movie's extras.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:22.050
The toughest challenge of filming in Austria
was the lack of ice and snow due to the unseasonal

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.250
weather, which delayed preparations.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:29.120
In fact, to cover the hillside, the production
had to make 400 tonnes of fake snow.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.090
The exterior of the Hoffler Klinik was filmed
at the ICE-Q, a restaurant at the top of a

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:37.000
3000-metre mountain in Sölden, Austria.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.720
However, scenes inside the Klinik were filmed
on a set built at Pinewood Studios.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.260
The Austrian action sequence featuring an
aeroplane and Range Rovers, was especially

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.260
complex.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:51.860
While only one airplane is seen on screen,
in reality special effects supervisor Chris

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.340
Corbould needed eight different planes to
pull off the stunt.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:03:00.040
Two of the planes could fly, another two were
fitted to a wire rig, and four others were

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.840
just carcasses with skidoos inside them which
the stunt team used to control the planes.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.810
For this action scene, the crew also built
10 sheds and a barn.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:13.790
Eight of the sheds were bought in the local
mountains and rebuilt on set, while the remaining

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.290
sheds and barn were built from scratch using
20 miles of reclaimed wood.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.660
The toughest location for Spectre's filmmakers
was Morocco.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.710
As well as filming in the cities of Tangier
and Oujda, Spectre also shot outside Erfoud

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:31.890
in the Sahara desert, which meant that the
movie's locations department had to drive

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:36.390
out to let everyone within a 20-mile radius
know there were going to be loud explosions

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:37.530
during filming.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:42.130
On the first day of the shoot in Erfoud, a
big sand storm, with winds up to 50 miles

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:46.160
an hour, meant there was no visibility so
the filmmakers had to halt production for

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.190
the whole afternoon.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:52.100
As for the temperature during filming, the
average in Erfoud was over 110 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.540
and, on the hottest day, it was 122 degrees.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.280
And as if it wasn't already hot enough in
the desert, the movie's special effects team

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.410
used more than 2,100 gallons of kerosene to
create what special effects supervisor Chris

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:10.730
Corbould, who's worked on 14 Bond movies,
says is the biggest explosion of his career.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:14.880
According to producer Barbara Broccoli and
production designer Dennis Gassner, one of

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.090
the most difficult sets to get right was Bond's
London apartment because everyone has their

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:22.800
own idea of the kind of place 007 would live.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.530
And because it says so much about Bond's character,
Daniel Craig was especially involved in the

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.040
design of that set.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.150
The scheduling of the high-speed boat chase
was particularly complicated because of all

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.310
the events in London at the time, including
the General Election and Trooping the Colour.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:42.160
The scene involved six night-time shoots and
low-flying helicopters, which meant the filmmakers

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.510
had to send 11,000 letters to notify residents
and businesses in the area.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:51.280
But the biggest challenge was lighting the
River Thames, which involved lighting 17 arches

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.600
on 3 different bridges as well as 10 rooftops
along the banks of the river.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.970
The filmmakers also worked with the House
of Commons, County Hall and The London Eye

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:04.440
to either change the colour of their lights
or make sure they had their lights on or off,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.440
as needed.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.620
Each night-time shoot involved a location
team of nearly 200 people, from security to

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:10.620
traffic management.

